{
  "passage": "Salt is not mined only from the ground. Along the coasts of Gujarat and Tamil Nadu, a lot of our common salt is obtained from sea water. Sea water is let into shallow pits called salt pans and left there. The heat of the sun slowly turns the water into vapour, a process called evaporation, and the salt is left behind as white crystals. This salt is then collected and purified. Evaporation is one of several methods used to separate the parts of a mixture. If a mixture contains a solid that does not dissolve in a liquid, such as sand in water, the sand can first be allowed to settle at the bottom. This is called sedimentation. The clear water above can then be poured off gently into another vessel without disturbing the sand, which is called decantation. To remove the last fine particles, the water can be passed through filter paper. This is filtration. In villages, husk is separated from grain by winnowing, in which the mixture is dropped from a height in the wind, and the light husk is blown away while the heavier grain falls straight down. Stones are picked out of rice and dal by hand, which is called handpicking. Sieving is used in flour mills and at construction sites to separate fine particles from larger ones.",
